										"title": "Cutover stage - AWS Prescriptive Guidance",
										"url": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/best-practices-migration-cutover/cutover-stage.html?utm_source=openai"
									}
								],
								"logprobs": [],
								"text": "1. **Copy data while the old database serves traffic.** Keep the old database as the only place the API writes. Copy existing records in batches and use change data capture (CDC) to replicate writes that arrive during the copy. Test at peak traffic and slow the copy if API latency exceeds its target or replication lag keeps growing. ([docs.aws.amazon.com](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/prescriptive-guidance/latest/strategy-database-migration/strategy-database-migration.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\n2. **Test what clients would see, not just whether the rows match.** Validate the copied records, then run a sample of real read requests against both databases while returning only the old database\u2019s response. Compare results and latency. Investigate mismatches after accounting for replication lag; expand reads on the new database only when it meets the endpoint\u2019s correctness and latency requirements. ([stripe.com](https://stripe.com/blog/online-migrations))\n\n3. **Rehearse the write switch and the rollback.** For a single-writer migration, briefly pause all writers\u2014including background jobs\u2014and verify that the new database has applied the last committed transaction before redirecting writes. Decide in advance who can call a rollback and what triggers it. Once the new database accepts writes, switching traffic back is not enough: first synchronize those writes back to the old database, or fix the issue on the new one. ([docs.aws.amazon.com](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/best-practices-migration-cutover/cutover-stage.html?utm_source=openai))"
							}
